From: Patrick Quinn <patrick@jquinn.com>
Date: 2026-06-23
Subject: [PATCH] redpine: fix kthread function signatures for kCFI

The out-of-tree Silicon Labs Redpine RS9116 driver (drivers/net/wireless/
redpine/) declares its kthread functions with the wrong signature:

    static void rsi_tx_scheduler_thread(struct rsi_common *common)
    static void rsi_coex_scheduler_thread(struct rsi_common *common)
    void        rsi_sdio_rx_thread(struct rsi_common *common)

then passes them to kthread_run() through rsi_create_kthread()'s `void *
func_ptr` parameter. kthread_run() expects `int (*)(void *)`. The implicit
cast through a `void *` parameter slot bypasses the C type system but
NOT kCFI: at runtime, kthread() calls the function pointer with a kCFI
hash check for `int (*)(void *)` (0x89fb613d), the actual function has
the hash for its declared signature, and the check fires.

With CONFIG_CFI_CLANG=y and CONFIG_CFI_PERMISSIVE=n (Alpine's default
linux-purism-librem5 build), each mismatch is "Internal error: Oops -
CFI" — the offending thread is killed. On the Librem 5 with an RS9116
M.2 card, three threads die at driver load:

    CFI failure at kthread+0x124/0x1cc (target: rsi_tx_scheduler_thread)
    CFI failure at kthread+0x124/0x1cc (target: rsi_coex_scheduler_thread)
    CFI failure at kthread+0x124/0x1cc (target: rsi_sdio_rx_thread)

The driver itself appears to come up — firmware loads, BT side works via
the BT subsystem path — but no wlan0 ever registers because the TX and
RX paths are gone. NetworkManager reports WIFI-HW: missing.

Fix is the standard kthread idiom: declare each function as
`int func(void *data)`, cast `data` to the real argument type inside the
body, and tighten rsi_create_kthread()'s function-pointer parameter to
`int (*)(void *)` so future signature drift gets caught at compile time.
The bare `return;` in rsi_sdio_rx_thread's error path becomes `return 0;`.

kthread_complete_and_exit() is __noreturn so no trailing `return 0;` is
needed at end-of-function — the compiler accepts it.

Same fix applies to the in-tree drivers/net/wireless/rsi/ tree (mainline
has the same signature pattern — `void func(struct rsi_common *)` with
the cast hidden in rsi_create_kthread). That tree isn't loaded on the
L5 (rsi_91x and rsi_sdio are blacklisted in /usr/lib/modprobe.d/rsi.conf
in favour of the out-of-tree redpine driver) so we don't patch it here.
Upstream fix should mirror this against drivers/net/wireless/rsi/ as well.

Signed-off-by: Patrick Quinn <patrick@jquinn.com>
---
diff -urN orig/drivers/net/wireless/redpine/rsi_91x_coex.c new/drivers/net/wireless/redpine/rsi_91x_coex.c
--- a/drivers/net/wireless/redpine/rsi_91x_coex.c
+++ b/drivers/net/wireless/redpine/rsi_91x_coex.c
@@ -87,12 +87,14 @@
 /**
  * rsi_coex_scheduler_thread() - This function is a kernel thread to schedule
  *			       the coex packets to device
- * @common: Pointer to the driver private structure.
+ * @data: Driver private structure (cast from void *) — kthread signature.
  *
- * Return: None.
+ * Return: 0 on thread exit (kthread_complete_and_exit is __noreturn so this
+ *         is unreachable, but kCFI requires the int(void *) signature).
  */
-static void rsi_coex_scheduler_thread(struct rsi_common *common)
+static int rsi_coex_scheduler_thread(void *data)
 {
+	struct rsi_common *common = data;
 	int status = 0;
 	struct rsi_coex_ctrl_block *coex_cb =
 		(struct rsi_coex_ctrl_block *)common->coex_cb;
diff -urN orig/drivers/net/wireless/redpine/rsi_91x_main.c new/drivers/net/wireless/redpine/rsi_91x_main.c
--- a/drivers/net/wireless/redpine/rsi_91x_main.c
+++ b/drivers/net/wireless/redpine/rsi_91x_main.c
@@ -600,12 +600,14 @@
 /**
  * rsi_tx_scheduler_thread() - This function is a kernel thread to send the
  *			       packets to the device.
- * @common: Pointer to the driver private structure.
+ * @data: Driver private structure (cast from void *) — kthread signature.
  *
- * Return: None.
+ * Return: 0 on thread exit (kthread_complete_and_exit is __noreturn so this
+ *         is unreachable, but kCFI requires the int(void *) signature).
  */
-static void rsi_tx_scheduler_thread(struct rsi_common *common)
+static int rsi_tx_scheduler_thread(void *data)
 {
+	struct rsi_common *common = data;
 	int status = 0;
 	struct rsi_hw *adapter = common->priv;
 	u32 timeout = EVENT_WAIT_FOREVER;
diff -urN orig/drivers/net/wireless/redpine/rsi_91x_sdio_ops.c new/drivers/net/wireless/redpine/rsi_91x_sdio_ops.c
--- a/drivers/net/wireless/redpine/rsi_91x_sdio_ops.c
+++ b/drivers/net/wireless/redpine/rsi_91x_sdio_ops.c
@@ -74,8 +74,9 @@
 	return status;
 }

-void rsi_sdio_rx_thread(struct rsi_common *common)
+int rsi_sdio_rx_thread(void *data)
 {
+	struct rsi_common *common = data;
 	struct rsi_hw *adapter = common->priv;
 	struct rsi_91x_sdiodev *sdev = adapter->rsi_dev;
 	struct sk_buff *skb;
@@ -99,7 +100,7 @@
 			if (status) {
 				redpine_dbg(ERR_ZONE, "Failed to read the packet\n");
 				dev_kfree_skb(skb);
-				return;
+				return 0;
 			}
 			dev_kfree_skb(skb);
 			if (sdev->rx_q.num_rx_pkts > 0)
diff -urN orig/drivers/net/wireless/redpine/rsi_common.h new/drivers/net/wireless/redpine/rsi_common.h
--- a/drivers/net/wireless/redpine/rsi_common.h
+++ b/drivers/net/wireless/redpine/rsi_common.h
@@ -73,7 +73,7 @@

 static inline int rsi_create_kthread(struct rsi_common *common,
 				     struct rsi_thread *thread,
-				     void *func_ptr,
+				     int (*func_ptr)(void *),
 				     u8 *name)
 {
 	init_completion(&thread->completion);
diff -urN orig/drivers/net/wireless/redpine/rsi_sdio.h new/drivers/net/wireless/redpine/rsi_sdio.h
--- a/drivers/net/wireless/redpine/rsi_sdio.h
+++ b/drivers/net/wireless/redpine/rsi_sdio.h
@@ -185,5 +185,5 @@
 int rsi_sdio_determine_event_timeout(struct rsi_hw *adapter);
 int rsi_sdio_check_buffer_status(struct rsi_hw *adapter, u8 q_num);
 int rsi_read_intr_status_reg(struct rsi_hw *adapter);
-void rsi_sdio_rx_thread(struct rsi_common *common);
+int rsi_sdio_rx_thread(void *data);
 #endif
